(a) (a) Verification activities. Verification activities must include, as appropriate to the nature of the preventive control and its role in the facility's food safety system:
(a)(1) (1) Validation in accordance with § 117.160.
(a)(2) (2) Verification that monitoring is being conducted as required by § 117.140 (and in accordance with § 117.145).
(a)(3) (3) Verification that appropriate decisions about corrective actions are being made as required by § 117.140 (and in accordance with § 117.150).
(a)(4) (4) Verification of implementation and effectiveness in accordance with § 117.165; and
(a)(5) (5) Reanalysis in accordance with § 117.170.
(b) (b) Documentation. All verification activities conducted in accordance with this section must be documented in records.

[21 CFR 117.160] Validation.
(a) (a) You must validate that the preventive controls identified and implemented in accordance with § 117.135 are adequate to control the hazard as appropriate to the nature of the preventive control and its role in the facility's food safety system.
(b) (b) The validation of the preventive controls:
(b)(1) (1) Must be performed (or overseen) by a preventive controls qualified individual:
(b)(1)(i)(A) (i)(A) Prior to implementation of the food safety plan; or
(b)(1)(i)(B) (B) When necessary to demonstrate the control measures can be implemented as designed:
(b)(1)(i)(B)(1) (1) Within 90 calendar days after production of the applicable food first begins; or
(b)(1)(i)(B)(2) (2) Within a reasonable timeframe, provided that the preventive controls qualified individual prepares (or oversees the preparation of) a written justification for a timeframe that exceeds 90 calendar days after production of the applicable food first begins;
(b)(1)(ii) (ii) Whenever a change to a control measure or combination of control measures could impact whether the control measure or combination of control measures, when properly implemented, will effectively control the hazards; and
(b)(1)(iii) (iii) Whenever a reanalysis of the food safety plan reveals the need to do so;
(b)(2) (2) Must include obtaining and evaluating scientific and technical evidence (or, when such evidence is not available or is inadequate, conducting studies) to determine whether the preventive controls, when properly implemented, will effectively control the hazards; and
(c) (c) You do not need to validate:
(c)(1) (1) The food allergen controls in § 117.135(c)(2);
(c)(2) (2) The sanitation controls in § 117.135(c)(3);
(c)(3) (3) The recall plan in § 117.139;
(c)(4) (4) The supply-chain program in subpart G of this part; and
(c)(5) (5) Other preventive controls, if the preventive controls qualified individual prepares (or oversees the preparation of) a written justification that validation is not applicable based on factors such as the nature of the hazard, and the nature of the preventive control and its role in the facility's food safety system.

[21 CFR 117.165] Verification of implementation and effectiveness.
(a) (a) Verification activities. You must verify that the preventive controls are consistently implemented and are effectively and significantly minimizing or preventing the hazards. To do so you must conduct activities that include the following, as appropriate to the facility, the food, and the nature of the preventive control and its role in the facility's food safety system:
(a)(1) (1) Calibration of process monitoring instruments and verification instruments (or checking them for accuracy);
(a)(2) (2) Product testing, for a pathogen (or appropriate indicator organism) or other hazard;
(a)(3) (3) Environmental monitoring, for an environmental pathogen or for an appropriate indicator organism, if contamination of a ready-to-eat food with an environmental pathogen is a hazard requiring a preventive control, by collecting and testing environmental samples; and
(a)(4) (4) Review of the following records within the specified timeframes, by (or under the oversight of) a preventive controls qualified individual, to ensure that the records are complete, the activities reflected in the records occurred in accordance with the food safety plan, the preventive controls are effective, and appropriate decisions were made about corrective actions:
(a)(4)(i) (i) Records of monitoring and corrective action records within 7 working days after the records are created or within a reasonable timeframe, provided that the preventive controls qualified individual prepares (or oversees the preparation of) a written justification for a timeframe that exceeds 7 working days; and
(a)(4)(ii) (ii) Records of calibration, testing (e.g., product testing, environmental monitoring), supplier and supply-chain verification activities, and other verification activities within a reasonable time after the records are created; and
(a)(5) (5) Other activities appropriate for verification of implementation and effectiveness.
(b) (b) Written procedures. As appropriate to the facility, the food, the nature of the preventive control, and the role of the preventive control in the facility's food safety system, you must establish and implement written procedures for the following activities:
(b)(1) (1) The method and frequency of calibrating process monitoring instruments and verification instruments (or checking them for accuracy) as required by paragraph (a)(1) of this section.
(b)(2) (2) Product testing as required by paragraph (a)(2) of this section. Procedures for product testing must:
(b)(2)(i) (i) Be scientifically valid;
(b)(2)(ii) (ii) Identify the test microorganism(s) or other analyte(s);
(b)(2)(iii) (iii) Specify the procedures for identifying samples, including their relationship to specific lots of product;
(b)(2)(iv) (iv) Include the procedures for sampling, including the number of samples and the sampling frequency;
(b)(2)(v) (v) Identify the test(s) conducted, including the analytical method(s) used;
(b)(2)(vi) (vi) Identify the laboratory conducting the testing; and
(b)(2)(vii) (vii) Include the corrective action procedures required by § 117.150(a)(1).
(b)(3) (3) Environmental monitoring as required by paragraph (a)(3) of this section. Procedures for environmental monitoring must:
(b)(3)(i) (i) Be scientifically valid;
(b)(3)(ii) (ii) Identify the test microorganism(s);
(b)(3)(iii) (iii) Identify the locations from which samples will be collected and the number of sites to be tested during routine environmental monitoring. The number and location of sampling sites must be adequate to determine whether preventive controls are effective;
(b)(3)(iv) (iv) Identify the timing and frequency for collecting and testing samples. The timing and frequency for collecting and testing samples must be adequate to determine whether preventive controls are effective;
(b)(3)(v) (v) Identify the test(s) conducted, including the analytical method(s) used;
(b)(3)(vi) (vi) Identify the laboratory conducting the testing; and
(b)(3)(vii) (vii) Include the corrective action procedures required by § 117.150(a)(1).

