[21 CFR 111.105] What must quality control personnel do?
— Quality control personnel must ensure that your manufacturing, packaging, labeling, and holding operations ensure the quality of the dietary supplement and that the dietary supplement is packaged and labeled as specified in the master manufacturing record. To do so, quality control personnel must perform operations that include:
(a) (a) Approving or rejecting all processes, specifications, written procedures, controls, tests, and examinations, and deviations from or modifications to them, that may affect the identity, purity, strength, or composition of a dietary supplement;
(b) (b) Reviewing and approving the documentation setting forth the basis for qualification of any supplier;
(c) (c) Reviewing and approving the documentation setting forth the basis for why meeting in-process specifications, in combination with meeting component specifications, will help ensure that the identity, purity, strength, and composition of the dietary supplement are met;
(d) (d) Reviewing and approving the documentation setting forth the basis for why the results of appropriate tests or examinations for each product specification selected under § 111.75(c)(1) will ensure that the finished batch of the dietary supplement meets product specifications;
(e) (e) Reviewing and approving the basis and the documentation for why any product specification is exempted from the verification requirements in § 111.75(c)(1), and for why any component and in-process testing, examination, or monitoring, or other methods will ensure that such exempted product specification is met without verification through periodic testing of the finished batch;
(f) (f) Ensuring that required representative samples are collected;
(g) (g) Ensuring that required reserve samples are collected and held;
(h) (h) Determining whether all specifications established under § 111.70(a) are met; and
(i) (i) Performing other operations required under this subpart.

[21 CFR 111.12] What personnel qualification requirements apply?
(a) (a) You must have qualified employees who manufacture, package, label, or hold dietary supplements.
(b) (b) You must identify who is responsible for your quality control operations. Each person who is identified to perform quality control operations must be qualified to do so and have distinct and separate responsibilities related to performing such operations from those responsibilities that the person otherwise has when not performing such operations.
(c) (c) Each person engaged in manufacturing, packaging, labeling, or holding, or in performing any quality control operations, must have the education, training, or experience to perform the person's assigned functions.

ideal_answer:
The difference is one of function and independence, and Part 111 states it in two places.

Separation of duties (21 CFR 111.12(b)). You must identify who is responsible for your quality control operations. Each person identified to perform quality control operations must be qualified to do so and must have responsibilities related to performing those operations that are distinct and separate from the responsibilities that person has when not performing them. That is the source of the independence: a person may hold both kinds of duty, but the quality control duties must be distinct and separate, so production work is not approved by the same person acting in the same capacity. Both groups are subject to the same baseline qualification requirement - each person engaged in manufacturing, packaging, labeling or holding, or in performing any quality control operations, must have the education, training or experience to perform the assigned functions (111.12(c)), and you must have qualified employees who manufacture, package, label or hold dietary supplements (111.12(a)).

What quality control personnel do (111.105). Quality control personnel must ensure that manufacturing, packaging, labeling and holding operations ensure the quality of the dietary supplement and that it is packaged and labeled as specified in the master manufacturing record. Their functions are approval and review functions rather than production functions:
- approving or rejecting all processes, specifications, written procedures, controls, tests and examinations, and deviations from or modifications to them, that may affect the identity, purity, strength or composition of a dietary supplement (111.105(a));
- reviewing and approving the documentation setting out the basis for qualification of any supplier (111.105(b));
- reviewing and approving the documentation setting out why meeting in-process specifications, combined with meeting component specifications, will help ensure the identity, purity, strength and composition are met (111.105(c));
- reviewing and approving the documentation setting out why the results of the tests or examinations for each product specification selected under 111.75(c)(1) will ensure the finished batch meets product specifications (111.105(d));
- reviewing and approving the basis and documentation for exempting any product specification from the verification requirements of 111.75(c)(1), and for why other testing, examination or monitoring will ensure the exempted specification is met (111.105(e));
- ensuring that required representative samples are collected (111.105(f)) and that required reserve samples are collected and held (111.105(g));
- determining whether all specifications established under 111.70(a) are met (111.105(h)); and
- performing other operations required under the subpart (111.105(i)).

So production personnel carry out the manufacturing, packaging, labeling and holding operations; quality control personnel approve or reject the processes and specifications governing that work, determine whether specifications are met, and do so under duties that must be distinct and separate from any production responsibilities they hold.

