For a single-entity or co-packaged combination product, 21 CFR 4.4(a) provides that compliance with all applicable CGMP requirements must be achieved through the design and implementation of a CGMP operating system that satisfies either the specifics of each set of CGMP regulations listed under 4.3 as they apply to each constituent part (4.4(a)(1)), or the alternative in 4.4(b) (4.4(a)(2)). The second route is the streamlined approach: demonstrate compliance with one system in full, then add the specified provisions of the other rather than operating both regimes.

Drug-based system plus specified device provisions (4.4(b)(1)). Where the product includes a device constituent part and a drug constituent part, and the operating system has been shown to comply with the drug CGMP requirements or the medical gas CGMP requirements as applicable, these must also be satisfied:
- general requirements and management responsibility - ISO 13485 Clause 4.1, Clause 5 and its subclauses, Clause 6.1, and 820.10 (4.4(b)(1)(i));
- design and development - ISO 13485 Clause 7.3 and its subclauses, with one or more documented processes for risk management in product realization and records of risk management activities maintained (b)(1)(ii));
- purchasing - Clause 7.4 and its subclauses (b)(1)(iii));
- analysis of data, improvement and complaint handling - Clause 8.2.2 and 820.35(a), Clause 8.4, and Clause 8.5 and its subclauses (b)(1)(iv));
- installation activities - Clause 7.5.3 (b)(1)(v)); and
- servicing activities - Clause 7.5.4 and 820.35(b) (b)(1)(vi)).

Device-based system plus specified drug provisions (4.4(b)(2)). Where the product includes a device constituent part and a drug constituent part other than a medical gas, and the operating system has been shown to comply with the QMSR requirements for devices, these drug CGMP provisions must also be satisfied: 211.84 testing and approval or rejection of components, containers and closures; 211.103 calculation of yield; 211.132 tamper-evident packaging for OTC human drug products; 211.137 expiration dating; 211.165 testing and release for distribution; 211.166 stability testing; 211.167 special testing requirements; and 211.170 reserve samples.

Medical gas variant (4.4(b)(3)). Where the drug constituent part is a medical gas and the operating system complies with the QMSR, the additional provisions are 213.84 testing and approval or rejection of components, containers and closures; 213.94 medical gas containers and closures; 213.122 materials examination and usage criteria; and 213.165 testing and release for distribution.

So the streamlined approach is a choice of base system plus a defined, closed list of additions from the other - not a free blend, and not an exemption from either regime.

